---
import KnowledgeArticle from '../../../components/knowledge/KnowledgeArticle.astro';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is a good DSO?', a: 'There is no single good number. It depends on your payment terms, your customers and your industry. A useful comparison is your own DSO against your own terms, for example whether customers on 30-day terms are paying in about 30 days, and how that trend moves over time.' },
  { q: 'Is a lower DSO always better?', a: 'Not always. A very low DSO can mean you are asking for payment up front or on very short terms, which may cost you sales. Treat it as a signal to understand, not a target to minimise at any price.' },
  { q: 'What is the difference between DSO and average days to pay?', a: 'DSO is calculated from your receivables balance and your sales. Average days to pay looks at invoices that have actually been paid and measures how long each took. DSO can move because of how much you sold or invoiced; days to pay isolates customer payment behaviour.' },
  { q: 'Should I use total sales or credit sales?', a: 'Use credit sales if you can separate them, because cash sales never enter receivables and dilute the figure. Many companies only have total sales to hand. Whichever you choose, state it and keep it the same from period to period.' },
];
---
<KnowledgeArticle
  title="What is days sales outstanding (DSO)?"
  metaTitle="What Is Days Sales Outstanding (DSO)?"
